# ⚠️ RETIRED. Do not enable.
#
#     systemctl disable --now aivoice-cache-warmer
#
# It kept OpenAI's prompt cache warm so no caller paid for a cold first turn:
#
#     cold    ttft 1198 ms
#     +2s     ttft  805 ms
#     +180s   ttft  950 ms
#
# Two things killed it.
#
# 1. The cache belongs to the ORGANISATION of the key that filled it. Once calls
#    moved to per-client OpenAI keys, a warmer on the platform key was warming a
#    cache no client call could hit - working perfectly, achieving nothing.
#
# 2. Warming per client would have put the cost on the client's own account:
#    ~1300 tokens every 120s per campaign, forever, whether or not anyone rang.
#
# And the measurement above is the argument against it anyway: the cache still
# held at 180 s idle. Any campaign busier than one call every three minutes keeps
# its own cache warm for free, and any campaign quieter than that is being paid
# for on the chance a call arrives. The original numbers were taken on an idle
# dev box - the one traffic pattern production does not have.
#
# What was given up: after a long quiet spell, the FIRST call's first turn costs
# roughly 400 ms more. Every call after it is warm again.
#
# cache_warmer.py is kept. It is still the cleanest way to measure prompt-cache
# behaviour by hand if the question ever comes back.

[Unit]
Description=OpenAI prompt cache warmer (RETIRED - see comments)
After=network-online.target docker.service aivoice-agent.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
# code from the checkout, venv from /opt - see the agent unit for why
WorkingDirectory=/srv/aivoice/agent
EnvironmentFile=/opt/aivoice/.env
ExecStart=/opt/aivoice/agent/.venv/bin/python /srv/aivoice/agent/cache_warmer.py
Restart=always
RestartSec=15
SyslogIdentifier=aivoice-warmer

# No [Install] section on purpose: `systemctl enable` now fails outright rather
# than quietly bringing back something that costs money and warms nothing.
